<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Live TV screen background.

  A flat surface. This file previously stacked two large radial "glow" ovals —
  a 260dp one bleeding off the top corner and a 320dp one off the bottom — over
  a vertical gradient. Three reasons all of that is gone:

  1. The glows were tinted with the old crimson brand colour, so on the warm
     beige surface they read as a dirty pink smear behind the channel grid.
     Recolouring them to lime only changed which colour the smear was.

  2. A screen whose entire job is to present ~40 pieces of artwork must not
     compete with them. Every broadcaster logo here is a different hue; the
     only background that lets them all read correctly is a neutral one.

  3. Two full-screen radial gradients are overdrawn on every frame of a scroll.
     On the low-end hardware this app targets that is a measurable cost for
     decoration the user never notices.

  Depth on this screen now comes from the cards themselves — surface_raised
  against surface_base, plus a hairline outline — which is the same mechanism
  used on every other screen and works identically in light and dark.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <solid android:color="@color/surface_base" />
</shape>
